00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.960
And I think just watching from
the announcements to the growth

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.590
has being really, really good.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.640
So a year ago-- just a little
flashback for those of you who

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.630
have not been here--

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.390
a year ago, we talked about
the fact that already then,

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.850
in fact, probably even
before then, Kubernetes

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.190
was the de facto platform
for running containers

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.512
at a lot of different places.

00:01:32.512 --> 00:01:33.970
Like, at that point,
it was already

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.220
hard to find a cloud
service provider that

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.250
was not offering Kubernetes
as a service at that time.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:42.220
And that speaks to the kind of
proliferation of that platform

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.210
everywhere.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.000
We also at the time
when we started

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.710
kicking off thinking
about Knative,

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.890
we were looking across
the community and we saw--

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.670
the number is 13
here, but frankly,

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.660
who knows how many there
really were out there--

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.030
different projects that
were focused on building

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.370
serverless offering
or serverless platform

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.360
on top of Kubernetes in
an open source community.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.860
And the interesting
part was that they

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:12.190
were focusing a lot of attention
on very complex and kind of,

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.260
to some degree, almost
boring problems that

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.470
had to be addressed with regards
to running inside of Kubernetes

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.140
for different types of workloads
that Kubernetes was originally

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.260
designed for.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.390
So in addition to kind of
trying to solve some of the user

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.730
experience, they had to
address the building of images,

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.550
the routing, managing
the scaling, and stuff

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:36.540
like that, stuff that was not
really exposed to the user.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.180
In the process of doing that, in
the process of really building

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.540
all these new platforms, because
if you ask four engineers to do

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.950
something, there will be
five different ways to do it,

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.240
they end up building
very hard-to-reproduce or

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.630
nonportable solutions
that were hard to leverage

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.500
for the community.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:59.020
So not only they were
decreasing the portability

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.030
of those projects
by working together

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.520
on the same set of
problems, they were also--

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.687
given that the resources,
the engineering resources,

00:03:05.687 --> 00:03:07.000
are limited--

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.310
they were not investing a lot
of attention towards areas

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.490
that the developer actually
could benefit from.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:15.560
So the overall
experience, the new event

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.710
sources, the different
run times, and so forth.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:24.670
So at last Next, we announced
Knative as an open source--

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.260
set of open source
Building Blocks

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.430
for building a serverless
solution on Kubernetes.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.873
And I stress the Building
Blocks, because initially we

00:03:32.873 --> 00:03:35.290
were really focusing on making
sure that each one of those

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.760
can be used independently
to allow developers to build

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.700
products for their developers.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:49.510
And the initial set of work
was around Knative buildings,

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.440
eventing, and serving.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.470
So let's kind of look
where we are today.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.350
Today, I'm happy to announce
that not only has Building

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.080
Blocks been very successful
and continue evolving,

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.570
but there is now a series
of commercial offerings

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:04.810
that are based on top of
Knative Building Blocks.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.330
Different companies that are
listed over here, including you

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.050
might have heard this week about
the exciting launch of Cloud

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.232
Run and Cloud Run on GKE that
we have talked about over here.

00:04:14.232 --> 00:04:15.940
But in addition to
that, there's a number

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.640
of other commercial
offerings, both as a service

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.350
and as a product that different
companies use the components

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.020
that Knative enables to
build their offerings on top

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.880
of Kubernetes.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.550
So let's go quickly and just
overview where we are today.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.880
I think what I'm
talking about here

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.740
is set of features
and capabilities

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.520
that we provide that
were available in a 0.5

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.900
release of Knative, which
landed just this week.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.370
And in fact, there
is a blog that

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.495
was published this morning
that you can kind of get

00:04:48.495 --> 00:04:51.110
a quick update on what
new features specifically

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.210
are on eventing that
were added over there.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.990
But in area of serving, what
are the benefits for developer?

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.320
First of all, and the one
that we hear the most about,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.900
is the notion of
auto-scaling, being

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.570
able to scale your
application and respond

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.800
to unpredictable usage patterns.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.760
So no longer thinking about
what my size of my audience

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.010
is going to be or going
in manually adding

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.590
nodes, but having that
solution or the system

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.170
that you're building on top
using Knative automatically

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.180
scale and address the demand.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:24.590
But also when that demand
is no longer there,

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.080
being able to scale
to 0 and allow

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.030
you to use the resources
in your Kubernetes

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.500
cluster for other
work that might

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.240
be important at that time.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.670
So really greatly benefiting
the overall utilization

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:35.760
of the cluster.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.420
The other important part that
we talk about a lot of times

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:43.760
is the notion of snapping both
the configuration and the code

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.660
at the same time and being
able to reliably use it

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:51.680
for individual deployments,
which is great, because you

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:52.730
write all perfect code.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.650
But when I have a
bug, I sometimes

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:57.860
need to go roll back
to last previous known

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.160
state or some version that
I know actually works.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.210
So being able to manage
revisions and configuration

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:06.920
as a unit is critical for that.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.490
Knative comes with a very
powerful tool underneath,

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.350
the Istio Mesh Network.

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:16.110
And sometimes it can be
pretty complex for developers.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:20.060
So Knative exposes that as a
set of very simple set of APIs

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:25.520
that allows you to manage
things like request path access

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.930
control or being able to expose
certain metrics from that Istio

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.580
Mesh, but not
having have to force

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.790
you to configure this manually,
which can be complex at times.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:39.510
And there's a number
of other features.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.180
The other part about
Knative serving

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.740
is that it is very pluggable.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.680
And what we mean by
that is that in case

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:47.420
of Google, what
we've done is when

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.460
we built Knative-compatible
service, the Cloud Run on GKE

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.650
and Cloud Run, we can integrate
with native services that

00:06:54.650 --> 00:07:00.470
are offered on GCP that offer
things like building so you can

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:04.370
build source code all the way
to Container or Stackdriver

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.430
or other services for data
persistence and analytics.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.640
In addition to that,
it's also extendable.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.780
So we've talked about
number of partners.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.352
The two I want to highlight
over here is GitLab--

00:07:14.352 --> 00:07:16.310
I think we have a session
later today that will

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.140
highlight one of their demos--

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.610
that use continuous deployment
and integration demonstration

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.520
of how they connect
to the Knative APIs

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.320
as well as Datadog for
some of the metrics

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.280
that you can do as an
alternative to Stackdriver.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.310
So exciting things in Serving.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:35.720
In Build, from the
very beginning,

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:40.688
Knative was able to off
a reproducible build.

00:07:40.688 --> 00:07:42.230
And that sounds very
simple, but when

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:46.330
you think about being
able to reliably-- sorry.

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.530
This morning, I'm
struggling with English--

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:53.250
reliably built images
from the same code,

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.430
including things like
timestamps and others

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:58.430
that give you the same
image over and over again,

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.048
it's something that
I think, if you've

00:08:00.048 --> 00:08:02.090
done some builds yourself
before you will quickly

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.810
realize it's not easy.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.700
The other thing that Knative
Build provides is series

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:13.040
of pipelines or different
language-optimized frameworks

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.240
that can be used to build
directly from source and either

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.180
result in an image that then
you manually deploy or actually

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.300
help you go all the way
from source to URL--

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.306
to live active URL.

00:08:25.306 --> 00:08:27.140
All of that is
done on a cluster.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.230
So there is no need
for Docker locally.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.190
There's no need for
cross-compiling.

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:32.960
So if you're working
on a Mac or Windows,

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:35.539
and you need to build an image
that's going to run on Linux,

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:39.394
which Knative runs today on, you
don't have to deal with those

00:08:39.394 --> 00:08:42.030
and remember all the extra
flags and how to do that.

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.400
And some of the dynamic
languages, for example,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.160
might have to benefit
from that too.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.290
And because this is
running as a service,

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.990
it's running on the clusters,
running on top of Kubernetes,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:55.190
you're benefiting from a lot of
the elasticity and being able

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:59.390
to use the resources when you
need them and dedicating those

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:01.520
resources to something
else when you don't.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.340
Again, loosely coupled,
you will see that theme

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:06.650
across every one of the
components of Knative.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.260
So in this case,
integrates with a number

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:11.210
of different CI/CD tools.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.730
I think if you've attended the
Cloud Run Solutions yesterday,

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.090
I demonstrated a Cloud
Build integration.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.220
We have Jenkins X,
a number of others,

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:22.310
including a new foundation
that we launched specifically

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.910
to kind of focus on that
that leverages that Knative--

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:30.670
originally launching in
Knative is the Continuous Build

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.360
Foundation.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.620
And there is growing
number of Enterprise tools

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.560
that are kind of popping
up here that provide

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.260
some very specific set of
features for Enterprise use

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:46.380
cases, like policy management,
logging, and so forth.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.635
So again, so Serving, Build,
and last but not least,

00:09:50.635 --> 00:09:52.760
always Eventing for some
reason ends up on the end.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.135
I don't know why
that's the case.

00:09:54.135 --> 00:09:55.880
We need to flip the
order sometimes.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:00.980
So Eventing, super exciting
set of add-ons in the 0.5

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.140
release that we just
launched this week,

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.080
kind of adding to the very
primitive set of objects

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.270
that we have had
underneath and adding

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:12.710
some easier-to-use constructs
like Broker and Trigger

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.630
that developers
can easily just use

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.730
by leveraging some
of the defaults

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.500
that Knative already exposed.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:22.170
What Knative Eventing
does, by default, is

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:26.160
helps you really orchestrate
the event sources and events,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.940
both on the cluster as
well as off the cluster.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.870
It provides a very declarative
way of binding to event sources

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:36.170
and connecting them to
either directly some consumer

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:40.063
of the sources but also building
very complex set of pipelines

00:10:40.063 --> 00:10:40.730
that you get in.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:44.180
Those little pipelines get more
interesting and interesting

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.040
as people get creative
with how you can

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.690
leverage those capabilities.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.760
Because it runs on
Knative and it's not just

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.010
a brand-new set
of things, it just

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:54.830
leverages the Knative
architecture and components,

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:58.130
you're getting the exact same
scalability and other scaling

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:00.180
capabilities that
you've seen in Serving.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.830
So you can build things that
are very nominal in a resource

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:05.810
utilization when you
first deploy them

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:09.110
and you use them but
automatically address

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:12.170
a larger number of
events when you need to.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:14.330
And all of that is
based with regards

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:18.560
to transport on standards,
cloud events for kind

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:19.887
of packaging the events.

00:11:19.887 --> 00:11:22.220
So what that means, that we
are able to attach ourselves

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:25.250
to a number of other
different projects

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.010
and event sources
that generate events

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.880
in this new open
standard for events.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.010
Again, loosely
coupled or extendable

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:36.650
so you can bind to
existing event sources.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:40.190
There's probably
28, 23, or something

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:41.907
like that of different--

00:11:41.907 --> 00:11:42.740
I know, I went down.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.690
I shouldn't have gone down.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.440
The list grows all the time.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:50.180
And you can see that full
list of existing event sources

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.740
that you don't have to
build, you can just consume,

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.480
on Knative website.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.080
But we also provide a very
easy way of extending those,

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:01.190
and there is a whole
session later today, SRV305,

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:03.830
that you can come to that is
dedicated explicitly to the new

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:08.480
Eventing sources and [INAUDIBLE]
is going to talk about the new

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:09.830
object models--

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:13.280
hopefully not
business-- and share

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:19.790
some of the hands-on ways of
building new event sources.

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:20.330
All right.

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:23.220
So that was about the
capabilities of Knative.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:24.830
Let's talk about community.

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:27.260
Frankly, we could not
have done this ourselves,

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.950
and there is a pretty
fascinating community already

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:33.630
behind Knative, starting
with the launch partners

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:35.340
nine months ago.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.090
So let's overview.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:39.800
Today, again, 0.5
already landed.

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.850
Our community has 49, 50--

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:46.130
I think more now
since yesterday.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:48.970
These numbers are probably
from Friday, I think--

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:52.845
50 different companies that
are contributing to Knative.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.942
Over 3.7 thousand
different pull requests.

00:12:58.942 --> 00:13:01.400
The number of contributors,
and what I mean by contributors

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.710
is not only people who
write code, but also people

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.460
who review code, people
who write documentation,

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:07.815
400-plus of those.

00:13:07.815 --> 00:13:09.440
The one interesting
number here is also

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.180
we have now spun off nine
different work groups.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.810
So these are kind of
specific area interest groups

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:16.340
that are, like, you know what?

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.200
I cannot understand
the entire Knative,

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:20.810
but in the area of auto-scaling,
I think I can contribute,

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:22.790
and I can be a
valuable resource.

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.100
And people from a number
of different companies,

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:27.540
including those who
would never write code,

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.000
that's contributed to Knative.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.560
Come in and join us on that.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:32.840
The number that I am
most interested about,

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:36.422
though, probably most proud
of, is that the momentum,

00:13:36.422 --> 00:13:37.880
it wasn't just when
we launched it.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.650
It continued being sustained.

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:43.010
So I build this kind of
20% month-over-month number

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:46.310
by aggregating the pull
requests, the issues,

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:50.600
the comments, the Slack channel
participation, work group

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.132
participation, just
seeing this line growing.

00:13:53.132 --> 00:13:55.340
Tomas is going to talk a
little later about what that

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:56.510
means in engineering terms.

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:59.340
It's just fascinating.

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:00.660
All right.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.055
The other interesting number
with regards to community

00:14:03.055 --> 00:14:05.430
is that, yeah, Google was
there in the beginning and some

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.050
of their launch
partners, and we are

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:08.910
extremely thankful
for those partners

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:10.710
and their participation in that.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.800
But just the long
tail of companies

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.970
that are now
contributing to Knative

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:21.300
is something that speaks to
the health of the project.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.210
One fifth of all
contributions to Knative

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.060
are done by the broad
community, people

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:32.100
who have less than 15 different
PRs, I think, at this point.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:33.528
There's a link to
the live stats.

00:14:33.528 --> 00:14:35.070
So these numbers
change all the time.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.400
You can look it up.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:39.420
But that's just fascinating that
this long tail of contributors

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:44.010
drives really the momentum,
because different companies

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.020
getting involved in
this project means

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:47.970
that the number of use
cases that they address

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:48.900
is also growing.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.193
And that drives features
back into Knative,

00:14:51.193 --> 00:14:52.860
which speaks further
to the capabilities

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:54.727
that we can do with this.

00:14:54.727 --> 00:14:57.060
I'm going to hand it to Tomas
to talk about engineering.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.435
TOMAS ISDAL: Thank
you very much.

00:14:58.435 --> 00:14:59.820
Thanks, Mark.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:01.500
Super exciting to
see so many companies

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.420
contribute to the system.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:06.030
And I think it
speaks to how useful

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.480
these small primitives
that we have built

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:13.050
and how useful these companies
find these primitives.

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.550
We see a lot of
momentum in Knative,

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.300
and we talked a lot
about the open source,

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:18.160
but we see this in
customer growth.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.640
We see this in partners
launching services

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:21.845
on top of Knative.

00:15:21.845 --> 00:15:23.220
And we're seeing
it in the number

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:25.800
of contributors to the
project and the amount of code

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:26.820
they write.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.900
So just in the last
quarter, we had 228 people

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:32.700
contributing to Knative.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:36.360
And these folks
wrote over 1,500 PRs.

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:40.620
That's five times more than
what we had in Q2 2018.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:43.420
So amazing growth.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:47.580
And my team at Google,
we're writing a lot of this,

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.380
around 61%.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.900
And we plan to
continue investing

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:56.040
in Knative to keep
this acceleration going

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:56.700
into next year.

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.947
But as an engineering
manager, you

00:16:01.947 --> 00:16:03.530
know that just writing
a bunch of code

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:06.080
is not enough to have a
healthy open source project.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:08.370
There's a lot of other
things you have to do.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:12.290
So what we have done
as well is to grow up

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:14.000
as an open source community.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.500
We have created a
steering committee

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.750
that's in charge of
the overall health

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:21.060
and long-term sustainability
of the project.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:23.360
And we have recently
invited non-Google members

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:24.470
to the steering committee.

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:26.750
This is how we create
the robust ecosystem.

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:29.780
It can't be a single vendor when
we have 50 companies that are

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.865
partnering for a shared goal.

00:16:32.865 --> 00:16:34.490
We have a technical
oversight committee

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:38.980
that's doing a similar thing
from a technical perspective.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:41.110
Other things that we
have done is related

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.540
to this promise of portability.

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:47.470
We want to make sure the code
you write for Knative will

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:52.610
work, of course on Cloud Run,
but it also works in Knative

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:56.990
to open source, in your own
data center, or on a competitor.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:00.200
And the way we do this is
to clearly, clearly define

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.800
the expectations of the
system and have tests

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:05.109
that verify that the
implementations meet

00:17:05.109 --> 00:17:08.740
those specifications.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:12.310
I also want to highlight that we
have software engineers working

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:14.109
on Knative, but
we also have folks

00:17:14.109 --> 00:17:16.660
working on test infrastructure
documentation, samples,

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:19.069
et cetera.

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:22.579
I'm really proud about
how solid the engineering

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:26.720
work is on Knative and
how good our releases are.

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:29.520
My team knows that I'm a
stickler for flaky tests.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.030
I really don't like flaky tests.

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:33.620
And for a long time,
we had 0% flaky tests,

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:36.470
which wasn't true,
because it rounded down.

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:39.260
So it was actually
0.04% test flakes.

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:41.330
But it was still what you need.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:44.770
If we have 200 people from 50
companies contributing code,

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:46.910
and you want to
have any reassurance

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.130
that that code
actually works, you

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.620
need to have a really, really
solid test infrastructure.

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:54.680
And that's something we
have invested in a lot.

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:57.530
Similarly, we have a team
writing documentation

00:17:57.530 --> 00:18:00.320
and samples so that your
developers at your companies

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.590
can read how to get started
with Knative from a developer

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:05.740
point of view and from a
contributor point of view.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:14.510
So what do we get with 400
contributors and thousands--

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:15.527
4,000 pull requests?

00:18:15.527 --> 00:18:17.360
Well, we have done a
lot over the last year.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:20.240
It's pretty incredible to think
that just a year ago, less

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:23.840
than a year ago, is when
we briefed a few partners

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:27.340
under NDA at KubeCon
in Copenhagen.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.750
And nine months ago, we
launched at Cloud Next.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:34.080
And we got a lot of
excitement, which is great.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:37.700
We were super excited to
see that we hit a nerve.

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:40.040
A lot of people had been
thinking about the same thing,

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:42.770
and they wanted someone to
come in and really, really

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:44.960
rally the community
around a shared goal.

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:46.418
But we also got a
lot of criticism.

00:18:46.418 --> 00:18:48.668
And that's even better,
because that means that people

00:18:48.668 --> 00:18:50.280
care about what you're doing.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:52.130
And we took this
criticism to heart.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:56.000
So we spent quite a lot of
time between 0.1 and 0.2

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.150
to re-architect the system to
make it more flexible and more

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:02.750
pluggable so that partners can
fit this into the environment

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:04.332
where they're working.

00:19:04.332 --> 00:19:09.030
So at 0.2, we sort of had
the foundation in place

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:11.110
to have something really useful.

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.020
And the product
shifted at that point

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:16.290
from addressing partner
needs, infrastructure needs,

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:19.620
architecture, to
addressing customer needs.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:22.050
And we saw that in 0.3.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:24.760
We had the support for the
container resource spec.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:29.850
And this is largely driven by
requests for GPUs and TPUs.

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:32.460
A lot of people want to
run machine learning models

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:35.250
or machine learning
serving on Knative and GPUs

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:38.120
make that a lot cheaper.

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:40.800
People wanted to run
private Knative services.

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:43.060
So we added support for
private or public services.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:44.810
So you can do, depending
on your front-end

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:47.593
is open to the public,
your back end is hidden.

00:19:47.593 --> 00:19:49.260
And you don't want
that to be accessible

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:51.500
to the broad internet.

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.320
And of course, improved all
the scaling, improved scale

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:55.580
to 0, all those things.

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:01.920
At this point, we also
changed our release model.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:05.430
So we're now every six
weeks cutting a new release.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:08.100
And that's how we get features
out to customers quickly.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.400
Of course, bug fixes, security
fixes go out every week.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:16.140
But major changes go
out every six weeks.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:18.820
And this allows us to take
feedback from customers.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:22.140
So in 0.4, the feedback
had shifted a little bit.

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:26.410
From 0.3 was, OK, we can't
do what we need to do.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:29.340
0.4 was, hey, I'm
running Knative,

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.370
and it turns out that upgrading
to the next version of Knative,

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.680
which by the way, you now
have to do every six weeks,

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:35.550
is really annoying.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:36.520
It takes a lot of time.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.060
So we spent a lot of
time between 0.3 and 0.4

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.670
to make that into a seamless
experience for people

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:43.380
running on top of Knative.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.600
And also because we run
Knative services ourselves,

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:47.302
Cloud Run NGK and Cloud Run.

00:20:47.302 --> 00:20:49.260
And we want to make sure
that those systems can

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:53.110
upgrade smoothly as well.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:55.320
We also added more
support for things

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:56.945
we hear from Enterprises.

00:20:56.945 --> 00:20:58.320
They are really
careful about how

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.630
they manage secrets for how they
authenticate to other services.

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:03.510
Kubernetes has support for this.

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:06.090
We forwarded the
support into Knative.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:08.550
Similarly, once you
start architecting system

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:10.763
with a front end and
private back end,

00:21:10.763 --> 00:21:12.930
so you want to have a really
efficient communication

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:13.500
channel.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:20.845
So we added the support
for HTP2 and gRPC

00:21:20.845 --> 00:21:23.220
so that you can have this
efficient communication channel

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:26.450
between your microservices.

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:28.950
And now, just last
week we cut it,

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:32.280
announced it today,
0.5 gRPC streaming

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.860
and then a big
overhaul in Eventing.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:37.245
So really, really huge
improvements there.

00:21:37.245 --> 00:21:40.830
At 1:15 today, we're going to
do a deep dive into Knative

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:44.100
Eventing, and I really
recommend that session.

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:50.850
So I'm going to invite
Ryan Gregg up on stage.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:53.700
And he's going to talk about how
you can use Knative on Google

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.410
Cloud to make your
developers more

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:56.760
productive for your business.

00:21:59.378 --> 00:22:00.420
RYAN GREGG: Good morning.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:01.270
Oh, hey.

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:02.640
Cool.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:04.200
So I'm Ryan Gregg.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:07.650
I'm a product manager on the
Serverless Team at Google.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.890
And this week we were really
excited to announce the launch

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:14.508
into beta for both Cloud
Run and Cloud Run on GKE.

00:22:14.508 --> 00:22:15.800
Yeah, where did that-- come on.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:17.540
Let's everybody wake
up a little bit here.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:18.040
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:18.770
There we go.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:20.410
All right.

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:23.193
I'm not below begging for
people to applaud for me.

00:22:23.193 --> 00:22:24.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:26.370
Last year, when I
announced Knative,

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:28.470
I got applause for building
Hello World in Docker.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:31.400
So, like, I'm all for it.

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:33.630
So Cloud Run and
Cloud Run GKE, these

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:35.370
are our managed
Knative offerings.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:39.300
And Cloud Run is a fully
managed serverless platform

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.390
for running containers on Google
Serverless infrastructure.

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:45.120
So you get the Knative API,
you get the Knative Runtime

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:45.923
Contract.

00:22:45.923 --> 00:22:48.090
But you don't have to worry
about the cluster or any

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.220
of the implementation
details under the covers

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:52.230
or even what version of
Knative you're running.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:55.950
You just get to call the API,
deploy a container, and go.

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.470
And the best part about Cloud
Run is that it's pay-for-use.

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:00.060
You only have to
pay for the time

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.627
that the request
is being processed.

00:23:01.627 --> 00:23:03.960
And the rest of the time,
when we scale the service down

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.550
or even if we don't, it's free.

00:23:06.550 --> 00:23:09.090
Cloud Run on GKE gives you a
lot more flexibility, though.

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:11.730
It's actually running the
Knative open source components

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:14.260
along with some additional
work on top of your Kubernetes

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:15.460
and in cluster.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:17.340
So you can go in and
use the Knative API

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:18.720
to deploy workloads.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:22.290
You can use the great UI and CLI
that we offer through G Cloud

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:25.013
and through Cloud Console,
or you can go into the API

00:23:25.013 --> 00:23:26.430
and do all of the
Kubernetes stuff

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:27.972
that you're used to
being able to do.

00:23:27.972 --> 00:23:30.150
You can access the
Knative API directly.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:32.580
You can program Istio
under the covers

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:34.982
to add policies to the
workloads that you're deploying.

00:23:34.982 --> 00:23:37.440
And you can take advantage of
the additional resources that

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:42.060
are available in GKE, like more
memory, more CPU types, GPUs,

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:45.510
and TPU accelerators, all the
greatness of compute that is

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:48.380
available on Kubernetes Engine.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:50.680
And then, of course, you
can use Knative anywhere.

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:53.550
So in addition to the managed
products through Anthos

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:57.650
or on-prem offering and on
basically any Kubernetes

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:00.210
cluster anywhere, of
course, you can run Knative

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:03.030
and have a great story for
being able to use our serverless

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.520
experience across all
three environments.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:08.975
So wherever you go, you can have
a great serverless experience.

00:24:08.975 --> 00:24:10.350
We also have a
bunch of customers

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:11.760
who've been using us
in the alpha program,

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:13.843
and it's been really
exciting to see the workloads

00:24:13.843 --> 00:24:16.150
that they have
built. For example,

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:20.790
we worked with Airbus
Aerial to do an alpha test

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:22.650
on Cloud Run on GKE,
and they used it

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:26.130
for doing real-time
processing of imagery that

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.740
comes from drones,
airplanes, and satellites

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.410
to help understand the
impact of disaster areas

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:32.923
and be able to respond quickly.

00:24:32.923 --> 00:24:34.590
And they're really
excited about the way

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:35.910
that the serverless
platform scales,

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:37.710
because as it turns
out, you can't really

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:40.060
predict when natural
disasters are going to happen.

00:24:40.060 --> 00:24:41.790
And you need to be able to
respond really quickly and have

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:43.723
the infrastructure
scale up underneath it.

00:24:43.723 --> 00:24:45.390
And with Cloud Run
and Cloud Run on GKE,

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:47.190
they really saw
some great benefits

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:49.320
for being able to respond
to natural disasters

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.282
when they occurred.

00:24:51.282 --> 00:24:52.740
So those are our
two different ways

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:55.250
of running managed Knative
on top of Google Cloud.

00:24:55.250 --> 00:24:57.000
We're really excited
to see what everybody

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:58.450
is going to be building
on top of them,

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:01.110
in addition to how you continue
to use the Knative Open Source

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:02.477
project.

00:25:02.477 --> 00:25:03.060
Thanks, Tomas.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:04.290
Thanks, Mark.

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:06.048
TOMAS ISDAL: Thanks.

00:25:06.048 --> 00:25:07.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:12.970
Really exciting to see that.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:14.900
To summarize what we've
talked about so far,

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.890
so Mark started by talking
a little bit about what

00:25:17.890 --> 00:25:20.110
Knative does and how it can
make your developers more

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:21.657
productive.

00:25:21.657 --> 00:25:23.740
Then we talked a little
bit about the broad uptake

00:25:23.740 --> 00:25:25.240
in the open source
community and how

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:28.210
we're trying to foster a really
healthy long-term sustainable

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:30.820
community for Knative.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:33.940
I talked a little bit about
what we did last year.

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:35.710
And I want to just
give a hint of what

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:38.740
we see happening in the
immediate future for Knative.

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:45.430
So we just cut 0.5, and
0.5 is a great milestone.

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:48.130
But the real important
milestone is 1.0.

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:50.350
That's when people can put
their mission-critical,

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.780
business-critical logic
on top of Knative.

00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:56.290
And quite honestly,
we're not there just yet.

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:59.260
So where I see the team
going and the community going

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.930
is that we really need to
double down on get to 1.0.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:05.170
What we have right
now, it's super-useful.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:10.000
It has enough functionality
to be a really viable product.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.320
But we don't really have the
performance and stability

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:17.200
just yet that you can put
your 1.0 brand behind.

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:18.850
The other thing we
need to focus on

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:22.360
is to get an API that we're
comfortable standing behind

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:24.470
for years.

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:27.070
We have made some changes
throughout the last year,

00:26:27.070 --> 00:26:28.340
based on customer feedback.

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:31.570
That's why you have a
0.1, 0.2, 0.3, right?

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:33.970
We're bringing those
changes in, and we

00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:36.880
have a really focused
working group right now

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.632
to get the 1.0 API draft out.

00:26:40.632 --> 00:26:42.340
And if you're interested
in contributing,

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:44.508
we really value feedback
in these working groups.

00:26:44.508 --> 00:26:45.800
And they're open to the public.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:47.260
And if you go to
our GitHub page,

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:50.080
you can see how you can
join these meetings.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:53.170
I touched a little
bit on specifications.

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:57.650
And the broader term there,
I would call it conformance.

00:26:57.650 --> 00:27:00.380
Ryan was talking about
Knative anywhere.

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:03.080
That only works if
we have a robust set

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.360
of conformance and
conformance tests

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:09.500
that we can run to make sure
that this Knative deployment is

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:12.530
conformant with the
Knative standard.

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:14.150
That's a big focus for the team.

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:19.370
If you really love writing
tests, we have work for you.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:20.850
MARK CHMARNY: Who doesn't?

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:24.050
TOMAS ISDAL: Just ping
me, and I'll send you

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:28.280
a bunch of tests to write.

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:30.860
So these are things
that we're doing.

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:33.800
Of course, we're
going to continue

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:35.150
to address customer feedback.

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:38.120
We're going to continue
to add features,

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:40.780
but that's not the biggest
focus in the project right now.

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:44.150
The biggest focus is to get to
1.0, because in my mind, that's

00:27:44.150 --> 00:27:45.170
when the fun starts.

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:47.150
That's when people
start using your things.

00:27:47.150 --> 00:27:49.670
That's when you have the
customer, the big, big customer

00:27:49.670 --> 00:27:50.400
growth.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:52.430
And the reason
we're doing this is

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:55.040
because we want to help
other people be more

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:56.580
productive in this business.

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:02.820
And that doesn't really happen
until we get to GA and 1.0.

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:04.553
So we talked a little
bit about last year

00:28:04.553 --> 00:28:05.970
we were seeing
that Kubernetes was

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:11.580
becoming the de facto platform
for running containers.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:14.900
And last year
Knative didn't exist.

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:18.020
I hope now, through the
keynotes and the samples

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:19.770
and the products that
Google has launched,

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:22.110
plus the five other products
other cloud providers have

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.870
launched, we have
convinced you that Knative

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:28.260
is becoming the de facto
platform for running serverless

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:30.150
and Kubernetes.

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:32.340
With that, we have a
little bit of time.

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:34.150
Oh, yeah.

